Complexity AnalysisTime ComplexityBest Case Complexity:It occurs when there is no sorting required, i.e. the array is already sorted. The best-case time complexity of merge sort is O(n logn).Average Case Complexity:It occurs when the array elements are in jumbled order that is not properly in ascending and descending order. The average case time complexity of merge sort is O(n logn).Worst Case Complexity:It occurs when the array elements are required to be sorted in reverse order. It means that we have to sort the array elements in ascending order, but its elements are in descending order. The worst-case time complexity of merge sort is O(n logn).Space ComplexityThe merging step of merge sort needs additional space is required that results in O(n) in terms of auxiliary space.When to Use Merge Sort?Merge Sort is best suited for scenarios where:Stability is required: When maintaining the relative order of equal elements is important.Linked Lists need sorting: It efficiently sorts linked lists due to its pointer-based merging.Sorting large datasets: It performs well even for large data, unlike Bubble Sort or Selection Sort.External Sorting is needed: It is useful for sorting data stored on external storage devices.If memory usage is a concern, Quick Sort or Heap Sort might be better options.ConclusionMerge Sort is a powerful and efficient sorting algorithm that guarantees O(n log n) performance in all cases. It is widely used for sorting large datasets, linked lists, and external sorting applications. Despite its memory overhead, its stability and consistent performance make it an essential tool in sorting operations.Next TopicAdd elements to Array in Java ← prev next →
